    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "A summary of results from some other line-cross studies is given in [[SEE_TABLE:9.5]]. In most cases, the parental lines are conspecific isolates known at the outset to differ (often substantially) in mean phenotypes. The main message is consistent with the Nicotiana results — differentiation of divergent lines almost always involves epistatic effects. We will see in Chapter 11 that epistatic interactions can sometimes be removed by a suitable scale transformation, but it is unlikely that this would be successful for all the tabulated studies."
